Notation and definitions. Let $n$ be a positive integer and write $\langle n\rangle=\{1,2,\dots,n\}$. For an $n\times n$ complex matrix $A$ and nonempty subsets $\alpha,\beta\subseteq\langle n\rangle$ with $|\alpha|=|\beta|$, let $A[\alpha,\beta]$ denote the minor of $A$ with row index set $\alpha$ and column index set $\beta$. A minor $A[\alpha,\beta]$ is called almost principal if $|\alpha\,\triangle\,\beta|=2$ (the symmetric difference has exactly two elements), i.e., $\alpha$ and $\beta$ differ in exactly one index. For $\alpha\subseteq\langle n\rangle$, let $A(\alpha,\alpha)$ denote the principal submatrix on rows and columns indexed by $\alpha$, and let $l(B)$ denote the minimum of the real parts of the eigenvalues of a square matrix $B$.

A real $n\times n$ matrix $A$ is called:
\begin{itemize}
\item a $P$-matrix if every principal minor of $A$ is positive;
\item a strict GKK matrix if $A$ is a $P$-matrix and $A[\alpha,\beta]\,A[\beta,\alpha]>0$ for every almost principal pair of index sets $\alpha,\beta$;
\item an $\omega$-matrix if it has the eigenvalue monotonicity $l(A(\alpha,\alpha))\le l(A(\beta,\beta))<\infty$ whenever $\emptyset\ne\beta\subseteq\alpha\subseteq\langle n\rangle$;
\item a $\tau$-matrix if it is an $\omega$-matrix and in addition $l(A)\ge 0$;
\item (positive) stable if every eigenvalue of $A$ has strictly positive real part.
\end{itemize}

Questions.
(a) Must every strict GKK matrix be stable?
(b) Must every strict GKK matrix that is also a $\tau$-matrix be stable?

